¶
'''animability''' (an″i-ma-bil′i-ti), ''n.'' [< ''animable'': see ''-bility''.] Capacity of animation; capability of being animated.

{{smaller block|An ''animability'' of body is acquired (if we may coin a word). ''W. Taylor'', Monthly Rev., LXXIV. 393. (''N. E. D.'')}}

'''animable†''' (an′i-ma-bl), ''a.'' [< L. ''animabilis'' (a doubtful reading), < ''animare'', animate: see ''animate'', ''v.''] Susceptible of animation.

'''animadversal†''' (an″i-mad-vėr′sal), ''n.'' [< L. ''animadversus'' (pp. of ''animadvertere'': see ''animadvert'') + ''-al''.] That which has the power of perceiving; a percipient. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|That lively inward ''animadversal'': it is the soul itself: for I cannot conceive the body doth animadvert.
{{right|''Dr. H. More'', Song of the Soul, p. 422, note.}}}}

'''animadversion''' (an″i-mad-vėr′shon), ''n.'' [< L. ''animadversio(n-)'', the perception of an object, consideration, attention, reproach, punishment, < ''animadvertere'', pp. ''animadversus'': see ''animadvert''.] '''1†.''' The act or faculty of observing or noticing; observation; perception.

{{smaller block|The soul is the sole percipient which hath ''animadversion'' and sense. {{right|''Glanville'', Scep. Sci.}}}}

'''2.''' The act of criticizing; criticism; censure; reproof.

{{smaller block|He dismissed their commissioners with severe and sharp ''animadversions''. {{right|''Clarendon''.}}}}

{{smaller block|We must answer it, . . . with such ''animadversion'' on its doctrines as they deserve.
{{right|''D. Webster'', Speech, Senate, May 7, 1834.}}}}

='''Syn. 2.''' Remark, comment, reprobation, reprehension.

'''animadversive''' (an″i-mad-vėr′siv), ''a.'' and ''n.''

readings.c1s2:
[< L. ''animadversus'', pp. of ''animadvertere'': see ''animadvert.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Having the power of perceiving; percipient: as, “the ''animadversive'' faculty,” ''Coleridge.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' A percipient agent. ''N. E. D.''

'''animadversiveness''' (an″i-mad-vėr′siv-nes), ''n.'' The power of animadverting. ''Bailey.''

'''animadvert''' (an″i-mad-vėrt′), ''v. i.'' [< L. ''animadvertere'', regard, observe, notice, apprehend, censure, punish; by crasis for ''animum advertere'', in same senses, lit. turn the mind to: ''animum'', acc. of ''animus'', the mind (see ''animus''); ''advertere'', turn to: see ''advert.''] '''1†.''' To take cognizance or notice.—'''2.''' To comment critically; make remarks by way of criticism or censure; pass strictures or criticisms.
{{smaller block|<poem>A man of a most ''animadverting'' humour;
Who, to endear himself unto his lord,
Will tell him, you and I, or any of us,
That here are met, are all pernicious spirits.</poem>
{{right|''B. Jonson'', Magnetick Lady, ii. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|I wish, sir, you would do us the favour to ''animadvert'' frequently upon the false taste the town is in. {{right|''Steele.''}}}}
{{smaller block|The gentleman from Lowell ''animadverted'' somewhat, last evening, on the delays attending the publication of the reports of decisions. {{right|''R. Choate'', Addresses, p. 374.}}}}
{{smaller block|='''Syn. 2.''' Of ''animadvert upon'': To comment upon, criticize, disapprove, reprehend, blame, censure.}}

'''animadverter''' (an″i-mad-vėr′tėr), ''n.'' One who animadverts or makes remarks by way of censure.

