animate, living (also aërial, consisting of air), < ''anima'', a current of air, wind, air, breath, the vital principle, life, soul: see ''anima''; (''b'') ''animal'', n., = F. ''animal'' = Sp. Pg. ''animal'' = It. ''animale'', < L. ''animal'', rarely ''animale'', a living being, an animal in the widest sense, but sometimes restricted to a brute or beast; hence, in contempt, a human being; orig. neut. of ''animalis'', adj., as above. In mod. use ''animal'', a., ‘living, animate,’ is inseparably mixed with ''animal'', n., used attributively in the sense of ‘pertaining to animals.’] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1†.''' Pertaining to sensation. See ''animal spirits'', below.—'''2†.''' Having life; living; animate.—'''3.''' Pertaining to the merely sentient part of a living being, as distinguished from the intellectual, rational, or spiritual part; of man, pertaining to those parts of his nature which he shares with inferior animals.
{{smaller block|Good humour, frankness, generosity, active courage, sanguine energy, buoyancy of temper, are the usual and appropriate accompaniments of a vigorous ''animal'' temperament. {{right|''Lecky'', Europ. Morals, II. 132.}}}}
{{smaller block|Faith in God is the source of all power. Before a soul inspired by this faith, the ''animal'' strength of a Napoleon or a Jackson is only weakness. {{right|''J. F. Clarke'', Self-Culture, p. 377.}}}}
'''4.''' Of, pertaining to, or derived from animals.
{{smaller block|It may be reasonably doubted whether any form of ''animal'' life remains to be discovered which will not be found to accord with one or other of the common plans now known. {{right|''Huxley'', Anat. Invert., p. 50.}}}}

